<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="z80:LE:16:default:default">
<!--
    kuna-1288 follow-on, P2 jump-table recovery (JumpBasic sanity check),
    option segmentize (default on).

    Under segmentize a recovered jump table with a row outside the load image
    is rejected and the jump stays a jump-as-call, so an x86 real-mode
    jmp [cs:bx+table] read through the spec's 64 KiB currentCS guess is not
    followed into junk (kuna-segmentize.xml #11-12). The Z80 processor spec
    declares a segment op too, but its unsegmented build never emits the
    segment() user-op, and a Z80 table often holds a row in RAM or the BIOS,
    outside the image. Such a table was rejected whole; only a jump whose
    target is worked out through a segment op is judged now, so the switch is
    recovered in both passes, as with the option off.

    Authored Z80 at 0x0000 (a in A, table at 0x20, its last row 0xf000):
        cp 4 / ret nc / ld l,a / ld h,0 / add hl,hl / ld de,0x20 / add hl,de /
        ld a,(hl) / inc hl / ld h,(hl) / ld l,a / jp (hl)
        0x10 ld a,1; ret   0x13 ld a,2; ret   0x16 ld a,3; ret

    Assert #1: the switch is recovered in both passes (pass two failed before
    the fix). Assert #2: the jump is never left a jump-as-call.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x0" readonly="true">
fe04d06f260029112000197e23666fe93e01c93e02c93e03c93e04c900000000
10001300160000f0
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x0" name="pick"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option segmentize off</com>
  <com>lo fu pick</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option segmentize on</com>
  <com>lo fu pick</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="segmentize-z80 #1: the table with a row outside the image is recovered in both passes" min="2" max="2">^  switch\(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="segmentize-z80 #2: the jump is never left a jump-as-call" min="0" max="0">jump-as-call</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
